\ Spitfire: the view through the windscreen. An eye has a position and an
\ attitude; one matrix product takes every point of an object into the eye's
\ terms (right, up, ahead), an edge with both ends behind the near plane is
\ left out and one with one end behind it is cut there, and what remains is
\ projected onto the view: the picture's centre plus the focal length times
\ right/ahead and up/ahead. A line is not drawn beyond the visibility, and
\ fades into the sky's colour with its distance, as haze fades it. Day and
\ night each set the sky's and the ground's colours, the lines' light, the
\ bombers' colour and whether stars show: by day a blue sky over green
\ ground and the bombers dark against it, by night a black sky with stars,
\ the lines at half their light and the bombers pale. The sky is the
\ background. The ground is filled below the horizon a pair of rows at a
\ time, at any bank: each pair of rows sees the ground along a straight
\ line, divided into land and water where the water's rings cross it. A
\ point of the ground is water when it lies inside an odd number of those
\ rings: the map's edges, the land's outlines, the lochs, the islands and
\ the rivers' strips. Every line is cut to the view's rectangle before it
\ is drawn, so an end close to the near plane, projected far outside the
\ view, is never drawn out. Drawing goes through deferred words, so the
\ window draws with its own line and rectangle words and a test draws into
\ a matrix.

640 constant view-width
view-width 2 / constant view-centre-x
\ the view's height and its centre's row, pixels: above the panel in the cockpit, the whole screen once the pilot is out
variable view-height
250 to view-height
variable view-centre-y
125 to view-centre-y

: view-rows! ( rows -- )
	| rows ^view-height ^view-centre-y |
	rows to view-height
	rows 2 / to view-centre-y ;
\ the view's focal length, pixels: the windscreen's 420, or a camera lens's when a photograph is drawn
variable focal-length
420 to focal-length
\ drawing a photograph: every colour turned to its grey
variable monochrome
false to monochrome
2 constant near-plane
30000 constant clear-visibility
250 constant n-stars

variable sky-colour
variable ground-colour
\ the colour of the ground filled below the horizon where no terrain is drawn: the ground's, or in Norway its land cover's at sea level, set each frame
variable level-ground-colour
variable water-colour
\ the colour the filled hills lighten toward at their tops
variable hill-top-colour
\ the fraction of its light every line is drawn with
variable lighting
variable bomber-ink
variable stars-showing
variable cloud-top-colour
variable cloud-base-colour
variable fog-colour
\ how far anything can be seen, metres, and the share of a thing's colour the haze takes at that distance
variable visibility
variable haze

\ broad daylight
: use-day ( -- )
	| ^sky-colour ^ground-colour ^level-ground-colour ^water-colour ^lighting ^bomber-ink ^stars-showing ^cloud-top-colour ^cloud-base-colour ^fog-colour ^hill-top-colour |
	0x5a7eb0 to sky-colour
	0x2c4a24 to ground-colour
	ground-colour to level-ground-colour
	0x9ab878 to hill-top-colour
	0x30527e to water-colour
	1 to lighting
	0x161a20 to bomber-ink
	false to stars-showing
	0xeef0f4 to cloud-top-colour
	0x9ca2ac to cloud-base-colour
	0x949ca6 to fog-colour ;

\ a dark night
: use-night ( -- )
	| ^sky-colour ^ground-colour ^level-ground-colour ^water-colour ^lighting ^bomber-ink ^stars-showing ^cloud-top-colour ^cloud-base-colour ^fog-colour ^hill-top-colour |
	0x060912 to sky-colour
	0x0a0d08 to ground-colour
	ground-colour to level-ground-colour
	0x262c1e to hill-top-colour
	0x0c1628 to water-colour
	0.5 to lighting
	0xd0d0d0 to bomber-ink
	true to stars-showing
	0x3a3e4a to cloud-top-colour
	0x1c2028 to cloud-base-colour
	0x14181e to fog-colour ;

\ the air: clear to the full distance with a light haze, or fog closing in to a distance, the sky its colour and no stars through it
: use-visibility ( metres -- )
	| metres ^visibility ^haze ^sky-colour ^stars-showing |
	metres to visibility
	metres clear-visibility < if
		1 to haze
		fog-colour to sky-colour
		false to stars-showing
	else
		0.8 to haze
	then ;

use-day
clear-visibility use-visibility

\ draw one line of the view in a packed colour
defer draw-segment
\ fill a rectangle of the view in a packed colour
defer fill-span
\ fill a disc in a packed colour
defer fill-disc
\ fill a triangle of the view in a packed colour
defer fill-face
\ the whole view as a bitmap; a bitmap laid over the view with its top-left corner at a window pixel; the window's pixels to the view's on each axis
defer capture-view
defer draw-view
defer view-pixels

variable eye
{ :position [ 0 100 0 ] :attitude 0 heading-attitude } to eye
variable camera
4 3 0-matrix to camera

\ set the eye and the matrix that takes [ x y z 1 ] rows into its terms
: look-from ( position attitude -- )
	| position attitude ^eye ^camera |
	{ :position position :attitude attitude } to eye
	attitude@right to right
	attitude@up to up
	attitude@forward to forward
	[ right first up first forward first
	  right second up second forward second
	  right 2 @i up 2 @i forward 2 @i
	  position right v-dot negate position up v-dot negate position forward v-dot negate
	] 4 3 matrix to camera ;

\ a packed colour dimmed to a fraction of its light
: dimmed ( colour fraction -- colour' )
	| colour fraction |
	colour 16 rshift 255 bit-and fraction * truncate 65536 *
	colour 8 rshift 255 bit-and fraction * truncate 256 * +
	colour 255 bit-and fraction * truncate + ;

\ a packed colour's grey, its luminance in all three channels
: grey-of ( colour -- colour' )
	| colour |
	colour 16 rshift 255 bit-and 0.3 * colour 8 rshift 255 bit-and 0.59 * + colour 255 bit-and 0.11 * + truncate to level
	level 65536 * level 256 * + level + ;

\ two packed colours mixed, a fraction of the first and the rest of the second
: blended ( colour other fraction -- colour' )
	| colour other fraction |
	colour fraction dimmed other 1 fraction - dimmed + ;

\ the share of its own colour a thing keeps at a depth, the rest the sky's: all of it to a twentieth of the visibility, then less, losing the haze's share at the visibility
: depth-light ( depth -- fraction )
	| depth |
	visibility 20 / to clear-depth
	depth clear-depth - visibility clear-depth - / 0 1 clamp haze * 1 swap - ;

\ the stars as directions [ x y z 0 ] above the horizon, spread over the sky by the golden angle with a little scatter
: star-directions ( -- matrix )
	[ 0 n-stars 1 do k
		k 12.9898 * sin 43758.5 * 1 mod abs to scatter
		k n-stars / 0.97 * 0.02 + scatter 0.01 * + asin to elevation
		k 2.39996 * scatter + to azimuth
		azimuth sin elevation cos * elevation sin azimuth cos elevation cos * 0
	loop ] n-stars 4 matrix ;
star-directions constant stars

\ the stars in the eye's view, a point each, every third one brighter
: draw-stars ( -- )
	stars camera matmul to viewed
	0 n-stars 1 do k
		viewed k 2 @i,j to ahead
		ahead 0.05 < if continue then
		view-centre-x focal-length viewed k 0 @i,j * ahead / + round to x
		view-centre-y focal-length viewed k 1 @i,j * ahead / - round to y
		x y 1 1 k 3 mod 0 = if 0xc8ccd8 else 0x6c7080 then fill-span
	loop ;

\ draw a line cut to the view's rectangle (Liang and Barsky's clipping), so an end projected from close to the near plane, far outside the view, is never drawn out
: draw-clipped ( x0 y0 x1 y1 colour -- )
	| x0 y0 x1 y1 colour |
	x1 x0 - to dx
	y1 y0 - to dy
	0 to entering
	1 to leaving
	[ [ dx negate x0 ] [ dx view-width x0 - ] [ dy negate y0 ] [ dy view-height y0 - ] ] to sides
	0 4 1 do k
		sides k @i spread to room to step
		step 0 = if
			room 0 < if 2 to entering then
		else
			room step / to ratio
			step 0 < if ratio entering max2 to entering else ratio leaving min2 to leaving then
		then
	loop
	entering leaving > if exit then
	x0 dx entering * + y0 dy entering * + x0 dx leaving * + y0 dy leaving * + colour draw-segment ;

\ draw the edges of an object whose points are already in the eye's terms
: draw-edges ( viewed edges colour -- )
	| viewed edges colour |
	0 edges size 2 do k
		edges k @i to i
		edges k 1+ @i to j
		viewed i 2 @i,j to depth-a
		viewed j 2 @i,j to depth-b
		depth-a near-plane < depth-b near-plane < and if continue then
		depth-a depth-b min2 to nearest
		nearest visibility > if continue then
		viewed i 0 @i,j to right-a
		viewed i 1 @i,j to up-a
		viewed j 0 @i,j to right-b
		viewed j 1 @i,j to up-b
		depth-a near-plane < if
			near-plane depth-a - depth-b depth-a - / to cut
			right-b right-a - cut * right-a + to right-a
			up-b up-a - cut * up-a + to up-a
			near-plane to depth-a
		then
		depth-b near-plane < if
			near-plane depth-b - depth-a depth-b - / to cut
			right-a right-b - cut * right-b + to right-b
			up-a up-b - cut * up-b + to up-b
			near-plane to depth-b
		then
		focal-length right-a * depth-a / view-centre-x +
		view-centre-y focal-length up-a * depth-a / -
		focal-length right-b * depth-b / view-centre-x +
		view-centre-y focal-length up-b * depth-b / -
		colour lighting dimmed sky-colour nearest near-plane max2 depth-light blended
		monochrome if grey-of then draw-clipped
	loop ;

\ the sun by day, due south 45° up, a midday sun of August at 56°N
45 PI 180 / * constant sun-elevation
[ 0 sun-elevation sin sun-elevation cos negate ] constant sun-direction

\ a face's light: its own colour's share kept facing away from the sun, the rest by how squarely it meets the sun; its outline a darker shade of the same, so the walls, roofs and towers stay readable once filled (the shares estimates)
0.55 constant face-shadow
0.6 constant face-outline-share

\ an object's faces, each turned toward the eye, from the farthest to the nearest: filled in the object's colour by its light and the day's, faded into the sky at the object's distance, then outlined in a darker shade; a face with a corner behind the near plane left out
: draw-faces ( object viewed -- )
	| object viewed |
	object@face-normals to normals
	object@face-centres to centres
	eye@position spread to eye-z to eye-y to eye-x
	eye-x centres 0 @j - normals 0 @j * eye-y centres 1 @j - normals 1 @j * + eye-z centres 2 @j - normals 2 @j * + 0 > where to facing
	facing num-elements 0 = if exit then
	centres camera matmul 2 @j to depths
	facing matrix>array ( index | depths index @e negate ) sort-by to order
	normals 0 @j sun-direction first * normals 1 @j sun-direction second * + normals 2 @j sun-direction 2 @i * + to lights
	object@centre eye@position v- v-length depth-light to kept-light
	viewed 0 @j to across
	viewed 1 @j to rising
	viewed 2 @j to ahead
	object@faces to faces
	object@colour to colour
	0 order size 1 do k
		order k @i truncate to f
		faces f @i to corners
		corners ( corner | ahead corner @e near-plane < ) any? not if
			corners ( corner | [ focal-length across corner @e * ahead corner @e / view-centre-x + view-centre-y focal-length rising corner @e * ahead corner @e / - ] ) map to places
			lights f @e 0 max2 1 face-shadow - * face-shadow + lighting * to light
			colour light dimmed sky-colour kept-light blended to fill
			colour light face-outline-share * dimmed sky-colour kept-light blended to edge
			monochrome if fill grey-of to fill edge grey-of to edge then
			1 places size 1- 1 do t
				places first spread places t @i spread places t 1+ @i spread fill fill-face
			loop
			0 places size 1 do t
				places t @i spread places t 1+ places size mod @i spread edge draw-clipped
			loop
		then
	loop ;

\ draw one object: its faces filled where it has them, then its lines on no face; else all its lines
: draw-object ( object -- )
	| object |
	object@points camera matmul to viewed
	object :faces null @or null? if viewed object@edges object@colour draw-edges exit then
	object viewed draw-faces
	viewed object@loose-edges object@colour draw-edges ;

\ the indices of the objects the eye can see: in front of it, within the visibility and inside the view's sides, by their spheres
: visible-objects ( centres radii -- indices )
	| centres radii |
	centres camera matmul to viewed-centres
	viewed-centres 0 @j to across
	viewed-centres 1 @j to rising
	viewed-centres 2 @j to ahead
	ahead radii + 0 >
	across sq rising sq + ahead sq + sqrt radii - visibility < *
	across abs radii - ahead 1.6 * <= *
	rising abs radii - ahead 0.8 * <= *
	where ;

\ === water ===

\ the edges of the land's rings, a row [ x0 z0 x1 z1 ] each; a ground point is water when it lies inside an even number of the rings: none for the open sea, one for land, two for a loch or river on land, three for an island in a loch
variable land-edges
0 4 0-matrix to land-edges

\ rings of [ x z ] points as a matrix of edges, a row [ x0 z0 x1 z1 ] for each, every ring closed back to its first point
: rings>edges ( rings -- edges )
	| rings |
	[ 0 rings size 1 do r
		rings r @i to ring
		ring size to n-points
		0 n-points 1 do k
			ring k @i spread
			ring k 1+ n-points mod @i spread
		loop
	loop ] to numbers
	numbers numbers size 4 / 4 matrix ;

\ a set of rings as the matrix of their edges
: rings>edge-set ( rings -- edges ) rings>edges ;

\ take a set of edges as the land's
: use-edge-set ( edges -- )
	| edges ^land-edges |
	edges to land-edges ;

\ take a set of rings as the land's
: water-rings! ( rings -- ) rings>edge-set use-edge-set ;

\ the regions of land, each [ centre-x centre-z edge-set ]; the one in use, the nearest to the eye, so a far region's edges cost nothing
variable water-regions
[ ] to water-regions
variable region-in-use
-1 to region-in-use

: water-regions! ( regions -- )
	| regions ^water-regions ^region-in-use |
	regions to water-regions
	-1 to region-in-use ;

\ use the edges of the region nearest a point
: use-water-near ( position -- )
	| position ^region-in-use |
	water-regions size 0 = if exit then
	0 to nearest
	1e30 to least
	0 water-regions size 1 do k
		water-regions k @i to region
		region first position first - sq region second position 2 @i - sq + to distance
		distance least < if
			distance to least
			k to nearest
		then
	loop
	nearest region-in-use = if exit then
	nearest to region-in-use
	water-regions nearest @i 2 @i use-edge-set ;

\ whether a ground point is water: an even number of the land's edges crossed by a line due east from it
: water-at? ( x z -- flag )
	| x z |
	land-edges x z 1 0 segment-crossings 0 > sum round 2 mod 0 = ;

land-outlines [ south-land ] concat water-rings!

\ the colour of the ground seen along a line of sight from a height, faded into the sky with its distance
: seen-ground ( colour sight height -- colour' )
	| colour sight height |
	height sight second negate / sight v-length * to distance
	colour sky-colour distance depth-light blended ;

\ one row of the view's ground, two pixels deep, from one reach across it to another (right/ahead), in a colour
: fill-reaches ( row low high colour -- )
	| row low high colour |
	view-centre-x focal-length low * + round to left
	view-centre-x focal-length high * + round to right
	right left > if left row right left - 2 colour fill-span then ;

\ fill the ground below the horizon, the water in its own colour. Each pair of rows sees the ground along a straight line; the rings' edges that cross that line in front of the eye, found by segment-crossings, divide the row into land and water, and which the row begins with is settled by water-at? at its end on the view's side
: fill-ground ( -- )
	eye@position to eye-point
	eye@attitude to attitude
	attitude@right to right
	attitude@up to up
	attitude@forward to forward
	eye-point second to height
	eye-point first to eye-x
	eye-point 2 @i to eye-z
	right second to right-rise
	view-centre-x negate focal-length / to left-reach
	view-width view-centre-x - focal-length / to right-reach
	0 view-height 2 do row
		up view-centre-y row - 1 - focal-length / v* forward v+ to centre-sight
		centre-sight second to centre-rise
		right-rise abs 0.000001 < if
			centre-rise 0 >= if continue then
			left-reach to low
			right-reach to high
		else
			centre-rise negate right-rise / to level
			right-rise 0 > if
				left-reach to low
				level right-reach min2 to high
			else
				level left-reach max2 to low
				right-reach to high
			then
		then
		low high >= if continue then
		right-rise 0 < if high else low then to reference
		centre-sight right reference v* v+ to sight
		height sight second negate / to distance
		eye-x sight first distance * + eye-z sight 2 @i distance * + water-at? to water
		right centre-sight v-cross to normal
		normal second height * negate to offset
		normal first sq normal 2 @i sq + to reach-squared
		[ ] to crossings
		reach-squared 1e-12 > if
			offset negate reach-squared / to share
			normal first share * to origin-x
			normal 2 @i share * to origin-z
			normal 2 @i negate to direction-x
			normal first to direction-z
			land-edges origin-x eye-x + origin-z eye-z + direction-x direction-z segment-crossings to distances
			0 distances num-elements 1 do k
				distances k @e to along
				[ direction-x along * origin-x + height negate direction-z along * origin-z + ] to crossing
				crossing forward v-dot to ahead
				ahead 0 > if
					crossing right v-dot ahead / to across
					across low > across high < and if crossings across add-last! drop then
				then
			loop
		then
		crossings sort to crossings
		right-rise 0 < crossings size 2 mod 1 = and if water not to water then
		low to cursor
		0 crossings size 1 do k
			crossings k @i to across
			row cursor across water if water-colour else level-ground-colour then centre-sight right cursor across + 2 / v* v+ height seen-ground fill-reaches
			water not to water
			across to cursor
		loop
		row cursor high water if water-colour else level-ground-colour then centre-sight right cursor high + 2 / v* v+ height seen-ground fill-reaches
	loop ;

\ the sun's disc: its true radius, 0.27°, about 2 px, in a pale halo of 7 px
0xfff8e0 constant sun-colour

: draw-sun ( -- )
	eye@attitude to attitude
	sun-direction attitude@forward v-dot to ahead
	ahead 0.05 < if exit then
	view-centre-x focal-length sun-direction attitude@right v-dot * ahead / + to x
	view-centre-y focal-length sun-direction attitude@up v-dot * ahead / - to y
	x y 7 sun-colour sky-colour 0.35 blended fill-disc
	x y 0.0046 focal-length * 2 max2 sun-colour fill-disc ;

\ the moon at night, where tools/moon-for-missions.py places it for the mission's time and place, [ azimuth altitude radius ] in degrees, or null: its true radius, about 2 px, in a pale halo of 6 px
variable moon-sky
null to moon-sky
0xf0ecd8 constant moon-colour

: draw-moon ( -- )
	moon-sky null? if exit then
	moon-sky spread to radius to altitude to azimuth
	azimuth PI 180 / * to bearing
	altitude PI 180 / * to rise
	[ bearing sin rise cos * rise sin bearing cos rise cos * ] to direction
	eye@attitude to attitude
	direction attitude@forward v-dot to ahead
	ahead 0.05 < if exit then
	view-centre-x focal-length direction attitude@right v-dot * ahead / + to x
	view-centre-y focal-length direction attitude@up v-dot * ahead / - to y
	x y 6 moon-colour sky-colour 0.3 blended fill-disc
	x y radius PI 180 / * focal-length * 2 max2 moon-colour fill-disc ;

\ marks drawn on the ground after its fill and before the objects: the clouds' shadows
variable ground-marks
( ) to ground-marks

\ a point in the eye's terms, [ right up ahead ], projected to [ x y ]
: projected ( point -- xy )
	| point |
	[ view-centre-x focal-length point first * point 2 @i / +
	  view-centre-y focal-length point second * point 2 @i / - ] ;

\ a triangle in the eye's terms that crosses the near plane: the part in front of it, cut where its edges cross the plane, filled as one triangle or two
: fill-clipped-face ( p q r colour -- )
	| p q r colour |
	[ p q r ] to corners
	[ 0 3 1 do k
		corners k @i to here
		corners k 1+ 3 mod @i to next
		here 2 @i near-plane >= if here then
		here 2 @i near-plane >= next 2 @i near-plane >= = not if
			near-plane here 2 @i - next 2 @i here 2 @i - / to share
			here next share v-between
		then
	loop ] to kept
	kept size 3 < if exit then
	kept ( point | point projected ) map to points
	1 kept size 1- 1 do k
		points first spread points k @i spread points k 1+ @i spread colour fill-face
	loop ;

\ a mesh of filled triangles: its points as an n×4 matrix of [ x y z 1 ] rows, its triangles' corners as three arrays of point indices, its triangles' centres as an m×4 matrix; drawn with a colour for each triangle, from the farthest triangle to the nearest; a triangle wholly behind the near plane left out, one crossing it cut there
: draw-filled-mesh ( mesh colours -- )
	| mesh colours |
	mesh@points camera matmul to viewed
	viewed 0 @j to across
	viewed 1 @j to rising
	viewed 2 @j to ahead
	ahead 1e-9 + to safe-ahead
	focal-length across * safe-ahead / view-centre-x + to xs
	view-centre-y focal-length rising * safe-ahead / - to ys
	mesh@centroids camera matmul 2 @j -1 * argsort to order
	mesh@first-corners to as
	mesh@second-corners to bs
	mesh@third-corners to cs
	0 order num-elements 1 do k
		order k @e truncate to t
		as t @i to a
		bs t @i to b
		cs t @i to c
		ahead a @e to depth-a
		ahead b @e to depth-b
		ahead c @e to depth-c
		depth-a near-plane > depth-b near-plane > and depth-c near-plane > and if
			xs a @e ys a @e xs b @e ys b @e xs c @e ys c @e colours t @i fill-face
		else
			depth-a near-plane > depth-b near-plane > or depth-c near-plane > or if
				[ across a @e rising a @e depth-a ] [ across b @e rising b @e depth-b ] [ across c @e rising c @e depth-c ] colours t @i fill-clipped-face
			then
		then
	loop ;

\ what lies behind everything: the sky, the sun or the stars, the ground and its marks
: draw-background ( -- )
	0 0 view-width view-height sky-colour fill-span
	stars-showing if draw-stars draw-moon else draw-sun then
	fill-ground
	ground-marks execute ;

\ every visible object of each collection as an item to draw in order of depth, [ depth :object object ], the depth its sphere's centre's distance along the line of sight
: object-items ( collections -- items )
	| collections |
	collections ( collection |
		collection@objects to objects
		collection@centres collection@radii visible-objects to shown
		shown num-elements 0 = if [ ] else
			collection@centres camera matmul 2 @j to depths
			[ 0 shown num-elements 1 do k
				shown k @e to index
				[ depths index @e :object objects index @i ]
			loop ]
		then
	) map [ ] ' concat reduce ;

\ a collection of objects with their spheres gathered for culling
: collect-objects ( objects -- collection )
	| objects |
	objects size 0 = if { :objects objects :centres 0 4 0-matrix :radii 0 1 0-matrix } exit then
	objects ( object | object@centre spread 1 4 array ) map flatten objects size 4 matrix to centres
	objects ( object | object@radius ) map vector to radii
	{ :objects objects :centres centres :radii radii } ;
